or B7B8B9, but I have none of B5 B6 B8 B9 in hand; only B2\u00d72.  \n\nFrom the unshown tiles, B2 has 4 left, so could complete pung easily; W5 has 3 left, also could complete pung; but sequences would require drawing specific neighbors (W4, W6 for W5; B1/B3/B4 for B2), which is harder.  \n\nChecking safety: history shows F2 discarded recently by across player, but F2 not heavily in demand. W5 has been discarded by across player once. B2 not yet seen discarded much. W8 has been discarded by me earlier.  \n\nFor now, the **least useful** tile is probably T7, since my suit focus is not pure yet, and T7 is already in a meld, making the extra T7 in hand redundant. But T7 might complete a pair later if I draw one more T7 (3 left in wall).  \n\nBetter to keep W5\u00d72 (potential pung) and B2\u00d72 (potential pung), and discard a single copy that doesn't help sequences/pairs much.  \nThat leaves candidates: W8, B7, F2, T7.  \n\nW8 and B7 might form sequences with W6W7W8 or B5B6B7 etc., but no tiles in hand to support that currently. F2 is an honor that gives fan if pung but unlikely.  \nF2's Prevalent Wind bonus is attractive, but with only 1 copy left in wall, it's improbable to make pung.  \n\nDiscard F2 if not going for honors, but since all my melds are T, maybe try to shift toward **All Chows** or **Half Flush** (T + honors). That would mean keeping F2. But to get Half Flush, I must get rid of W and B tiles completely \u2014 that means discarding W5, W8, B2, B7 first. But then I'd need many T/honor draws, which seems unrealistic in mid-game.  \n\nConsidering the game stage: many tiles have been discarded. T9 is gone (0 left), T8 only 1 left, etc.  \n\nFor tile efficiency, I should keep tiles with more copies left in wall to increase pung chance:  \n- B2 has 4 left \u2192 good for pair/pung.  \n- W5 has 3 left \u2192 good.  \n- T7 has 3 left \u2192 could become pair.  \n- F2 has 1 left \u2192 bad.  \n- W8 has 3 left, but single copy in hand \u2192 maybe keep for W sequence if later draw W6, W7? I have no W6 or W7 currently.  \n- B7 has 2 left in wall, could become pair.  \n\nMost useless right now is F2 (only 1 left), then maybe W8 (single, not near other W).  \n\n**Final choice:** discard F2 to avoid blocking hand and keep W/B pairs/pungs possibilities, but re-evaluating \u2014 F2 is Prevalent Wind, if I draw 2 more (improbable), it's 2 fan. But discarding it is safer and doesn't ruin structure.  \n\nBut perhaps even better: discard B7, since B2 is a better B tile to keep (4 left, could become meld), and B7 is isolated and has fewer sequence chances given my hand.  \n\nActually, **discard B7** seems best: B7 doesn't pair with anything yet, has few sequence possibilities without B5/B6/B8/B9, and B2 is better for B pung. Also, with B2\u00d72, it's better to keep B2 to aim for pung and discard B7 which is less useful.  \n\nLet's compare:  \n1. Play F2 \u2192 lose Prevalent Wind chance, but safe.  \n2. Play B7 \u2192 keeps F2 wind fan potential if draw more, and B7 is less useful.  \nBetween F2 and B7, both are singles, but F2 has possible fan if drawn two more. That's very low probability though.  \n\nGiven game state, most efficient is discard B7.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nB7 is isolated, not supporting any immediate sequence/pair in hand, and B2 offers better potential for a B pung. Keeping F2 preserves a small chance for Prevalent Wind pung, and it's a safe discard later if needed. W5\u00d72 and B2\u00d72 are the main potential melds, so keep them.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B7"}
