Previous player has [F4,F4,F4].  \n\nFrom history, many terminals and honors are discarded.  \nWe want to discard the tile that keeps our hand flexible to complete a 4th meld + pair.  \n\n**Tile safety:**  \n- B6: Next player played B6 earlier, so likely safe from him.  \n- W5: Across player played W5 earlier, so maybe safe from him.  \n- B2: Not yet discarded much (next player played B1, not B2). Could be risky.  \n- B8: Previous player played B8, so maybe safe from him.  \n- W8: Next player's meld contains W8, so maybe safe from him.  \n- T7: Across player played W7 not T7; next player's meld has T8 pung, but T7 not shown in melds. Could be dangerous.\n\nGiven all this, let's evaluate discard candidates:  \n\n1. **Play B8**: B8 is terminal Dots, no chow potential, only useful if we get another B8 for a pair, but B8 pair not part of a clear plan. Safe-ish (prev player discarded B8).  \n2. **Play B6**: B6 has chow potential with B4,B5, and can pair with another B6. Safer (next player discarded B6).  \n3. **Play W8**: W8 is terminal in Characters, no chow (W7 1 left, W9 0 left). Only pair potential. Safe-ish.  \n4. **Play T7**: Terminal in Bamboo (7), but we already have a T7 in meld, so another T7 could form a pair, but we need a pair elsewhere anyway; T7 is middling safety (not discarded much).  \n5. **Play W5**: We have 2 W5, so discarding one breaks a potential pair/pung. W5 is middle tile, can chow with W4,W6 (W4: 2 left, W6: 1 left) but not great. Across player discarded W5 so safe from him.  \n6. **Play B2**: We have 2 B2, so discarding one breaks a potential pair/pung. B2 could chow with B1,B3 (B1: 2 left, B3: 3 left).  \n\nOur hand has two natural pairs: W5 and B2. Keeping both pairs means we can try for Seven Pairs (needs 7 pairs \u2014 not possible with 6 hand tiles left) or regular hand with one pair + 4 melds. We already have 2 melds, need 2 more melds from 6 tiles.  \n\nIf we keep both W5 and B2 as pairs, we have 2 pairs (W5, B2) and 4 singletons (W8, T7, B6, B8) \u2014 that\u2019s bad because we need melds, not extra pairs.  \n\nWe should discard one of the pair candidates to aim for one pair + 2 more melds.  \nBetter to keep the pair of the suit where we can more easily form melds.  \n\nOur melds are Bamboo, so maybe try for Half Flush (Bamboo + Honors) but we have no honors.  \nOr try for All Chows (all 4 melds are chows). Currently we have 2 Bamboo chows. Our hand tiles: W5,W8,B2,B6,B8,T7.  \n\nWe could aim for mixed chows:  \n- B2,B3,B4 chow (need B3,B4)  \n- B6,B7,B8 chow impossible (B7 0 left)  \n- W5,W6,W7 chow (need W6,W7 \u2014 W6 1 left, W7 1 left)  \n- T7,T8,T9 impossible (T9 0 left).  \n\nSo possible chow: W5,W6,W7 and B2,B3,B4. That would give us 4 chows (2 existing + these 2) and a pair from leftover tile.  \n\nBut we need a pair. If we keep B2 as pair, then we need W5 in a chow, so we must draw W6,W7.  \nIf we keep W5 as pair, then we need B2 in a chow, so draw B3,B4.  \n\nWhich is easier?  \nUnseen tiles:  \n- W6: 1 left, W7: 1 left \u2014 both very scarce.  \n- B3: 3 left, B4: 4 left \u2014 more available.  \n\nSo easier to complete B2,B3,B4 chow than W5,W6,W7 chow.  \nThus we should keep B2 for chow, keep W5 as pair. Then hand: W5 pair, B2,B3,B4 chow (need B3,B4), plus leftover W8,B6,B8 \u2014 need one more meld from W8,B6,B8.  \nW8,B6,B8 cannot make a chow together (different suits). So we need a pung from one of them (e.g., draw another W8 or B6 or B8) or make a chow using them with new draws, but unlikely.  \n\nAlternatively, we could keep both W5 and B2 as potential pairs and try to draw into pung for one of the singletons. But we have 4 singletons \u2014 that\u2019s too many.  \n\nGiven the need to simplify toward tenpai, discard the most useless tile:  \n- B8 is useless (no chow, only pair, terminal)  \n- W8 similar  \n- T7 is terminal in Bamboo, but we have Bamboo melds \u2014 keeping T7 might allow T7 pair if we draw another T7, but T7: 3 left unseen.  \n\nBetter to discard B8 or W8. Choose B8 because it's Dots and we might aim for mixed suit hand, and B8 is more isolated.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscard B8 because:  \n1. It's a terminal with no chow potential (B7 and B9 out).  \n2. It has low pairing usefulness compared to W5 or B2.  \n3. It's relatively safe (previous player discarded B8).  \nThis keeps flexibility with W5 pair and B2 chow possibility.\n\n**Answer:** Play B8"}
